---
import ProseLayout from '../../layouts/ProseLayout.astro';
import ControllerIdentity from '../../components/privacy/ControllerIdentity.astro';
import ChildrensPrivacy from '../../components/privacy/ChildrensPrivacy.astro';
import PrivacyContact from '../../components/privacy/PrivacyContact.astro';
---

<ProseLayout
  title="lastGLANCE Privacy Policy"
  description="How lastGLANCE handles your data. Your chores and people stay on your device: no accounts, no analytics, and nothing sent to us."
  lastUpdated="November 14, 2026"
>
  <p class="lead">
    lastGLANCE tracks when you last did recurring chores and, with People, when
    you were last in touch with the people who matter to you. Everything you
    enter (chores, completions, people, their dates, logged contacts and notes)
    is stored on your device. This policy explains what lastGLANCE accesses,
    where your data can go, and who controls it.
  </p>

  <ControllerIdentity>
    <p>
      This policy covers lastGLANCE specifically. For our publisher-level
      practices and the glance-apps.com website, see the{' '}
      <a href="/privacy">GLANCE Apps privacy policy</a>.
    </p>
  </ControllerIdentity>

  <h2>Data we do not collect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>We do not collect personal information.</li>
    <li>We do not use analytics or tracking.</li>
    <li>We do not have user accounts.</li>
    <li>We do not store your data on any server we operate. (The web app may use a stateless relay for sync traffic, described below; it stores nothing.)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Note for the web app: the web version of lastGLANCE may relay WebDAV
    requests through a stateless proxy we host, because browsers block many
    direct requests. Your server address, credentials and data pass through it in
    transit and are never stored or logged. The Android, iOS and desktop apps
    connect to your server directly.
  </p>

  <h2>Data the app accesses</h2>
  <p>
    Every feature below is optional, and nothing reaches GLANCE Apps.
  </p>

  <h3>Phone contacts (People)</h3>
  <p>
    On Android and iOS, lastGLANCE can read your phone's contacts to import people
    or link a person to a contact. This is off by default. lastGLANCE asks for
    contacts permission only after you turn on Phone contacts in Settings and
    confirm an in-app explanation, and you can turn it off or revoke the
    permission at any time. Access is read-only: lastGLANCE never adds, changes,
    or deletes your contacts.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      When you import, lastGLANCE saves the contacts you choose as people, with
      their names and dates (such as birthdays), in the app's local storage.
    </li>
    <li>
      When you link a person to a contact, lastGLANCE remembers which contact it
      is on that device only. The link is not synced.
    </li>
    <li>
      Phone numbers and email addresses are read at the moment you tap Call, Text
      or Email, and a contact photo when you open the person. They are handed to
      your phone's dialer, messaging or mail app, or shown on screen, and are not
      stored by lastGLANCE.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Imported people leave your device only if you turn on sync, as described
    below.
  </p>

  <h3>Sync</h3>
  <p>
    lastGLANCE can sync to destinations you configure: a WebDAV server (such as
    Nextcloud), a GLANCEvault instance, or a folder that a sync tool you choose
    (such as Syncthing, Nextcloud, Google Drive, Dropbox, OneDrive or
    FolderSync) keeps in step across your devices. On iPhone, iPad and Mac it
    also syncs through your own iCloud account, which is on by default so your
    devices stay in step with nothing to set up; every other destination is off
    until you set it up. Your People data travels as its own file or vault
    collection, alongside your chores, and uses the same encryption as your
    chores.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      iCloud (iPhone, iPad and Mac): your chores and People are stored as files in
      lastGLANCE's folder in your personal iCloud account, protected by Apple's
      iCloud encryption rather than the app's passphrase-based encryption. Each
      device has its own switch in Cloud Sync; turning it off stops that device
      reading or writing iCloud and leaves the copy there for your other
      devices. When a fresh install finds your data in iCloud, it asks before
      restoring it. GLANCE Apps cannot see your iCloud data; Apple's privacy
      policy governs data stored in iCloud.
    </li>
    <li>
      GLANCEvault: your data is always encrypted on your device before it is
      sent. The server stores encrypted content only.
    </li>
    <li>
      WebDAV: encryption is optional and off by default. Turn it on in Cloud Sync
      to encrypt your data before upload. Otherwise it is stored on your server
      as plain text, protected by that server's own access controls.
    </li>
    <li>
      Folder sync: encryption is a per-device switch in Cloud Sync. The file is
      stored wherever your sync tool keeps that folder and is governed by that
      tool's privacy policy.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Your encryption passphrase is never stored. The key derived from it is kept
    on your device in its secure storage (the Android Keystore, the iOS
    Keychain, the macOS Keychain, Windows' protected storage, or the Linux
    keyring) so you are not asked for it on every launch. Where no secure
    storage is available (the web app, or Linux without a keyring), it is kept
    in the app's local storage. If you forget your passphrase, encrypted data
    cannot be recovered.
  </p>
  <p>
    Household: if you share lastGLANCE with your household, the members' display
    names are kept in a small shared file on your sync destination so other GLANCE
    Apps can recognize the same people. This file is not encrypted. It stays on
    the destination you control.
  </p>

  <h3>Connections to other GLANCE Apps and automation</h3>
  <p>
    These are off by default, and each one runs only through the sync
    destinations you set up for it. They send nothing to GLANCE Apps.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      dayGLANCE: lastGLANCE can send chores, a reminder to get in touch with
      someone whose catch-up is overdue, and today's birthdays and other dates to
      dayGLANCE as tasks, and receive completions back.
    </li>
    <li>
      lifeGLANCE: for people you choose, lastGLANCE can add each contact you log
      to your lifeGLANCE timeline.
    </li>
    <li>
      Automation apps such as Tasker (Android): other apps on your phone can ask
      lastGLANCE to add or complete a chore, log a contact or open a screen, and
      read summary counts such as how many chores are overdue.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These messages use the GLANCEintents protocol and can be encrypted with your
    sync passphrase. On iPhone, iPad and Mac they can also travel through iCloud, in
    a shared folder in dayGLANCE's iCloud storage in your account, if you turn
    that on in Integrations; messages sent that way are protected by Apple's
    iCloud encryption rather than your passphrase.
  </p>

  <h3>Notifications and widgets</h3>
  <p>
    Chore reminders, date reminders and the daily catch-up digest are scheduled
    on your device. Home screen and Lock Screen widgets read a summary of your
    chores and people (names, how long since you were in touch, upcoming dates)
    that the app saves on your device. None of this is sent anywhere.
  </p>

  <h3>Local AI assistants (MCP server)</h3>
  <p>
    The lastGLANCE desktop app (macOS, Windows and Linux) includes an optional
    local server built on the Model Context Protocol (MCP), an open standard
    that lets AI assistant applications on the same computer (such as Claude
    Desktop or Claude Code) read your lastGLANCE data and, if you allow it, make
    changes. It is off by default. In Settings, then Integrations, then On this
    computer, you choose what it may read (Chores, or Chores and People) and,
    separately, whether it may make changes. lastGLANCE shows you an
    explanation and asks you to confirm before each of these takes effect. The
    server listens only on your own computer (127.0.0.1) and is never reachable
    from the network. Applications connect with an access token that lastGLANCE
    generates; you can replace it at any time, which disconnects every
    application that had the old one.
  </p>
  <p>
    With Chores, an application you connect can read your chores, their
    categories and details, and their completion history, including any notes on
    completions. If you share lastGLANCE with your household, it can also read
    the names of the other members and see who a chore is assigned to. With
    Chores and People, it can also read your People: their names and nicknames,
    circles, dates such as birthdays, the notes you keep about them, and the
    contacts you have logged with them, including any notes. People is a
    separate choice because it holds information about other people. A
    connected application cannot read phone numbers, email addresses or contact
    photos, which lastGLANCE does not store.
  </p>
  <p>
    With changes allowed, a connected application can mark chores done, add and
    edit chores and categories, add and edit people and their dates, log
    contacts, and organize circles. It can never delete anything. It can make at
    most 30 changes a minute, and the changes it has made since lastGLANCE last
    started are listed in Settings, where you can undo them.
  </p>
  <p>
    On macOS and Windows, Set up Claude Desktop adds an entry for lastGLANCE to
    Claude Desktop's settings on your computer and leaves its other entries
    alone. While the server is on, lastGLANCE keeps its port and access token in
    a file in its own data folder on your computer, so a connected application
    can find it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Assistant applications typically transmit what they read to third-party AI
    providers over the internet. lastGLANCE itself transmits nothing. The
    connection is local, and any data that leaves your device is sent by the
    client application you chose, installed, and configured, not by lastGLANCE.
    lastGLANCE has no relationship with, and no visibility into, those
    providers, and cannot see, limit, or audit what a connected client does with
    data it has read. This is outside the scope of lastGLANCE's own encryption
    and no-server-access guarantees, which cover how lastGLANCE stores and syncs
    your data, not what an application you authorized does after reading it.
    You are responsible for reviewing the privacy policy of any client you
    connect (and the AI provider behind it).
  </p>

  <h3>Stream Deck</h3>
  <p>
    The lastGLANCE desktop app can work with an Elgato Stream Deck through the
    lastGLANCE Stream Deck plugin. This is off by default; you turn it on in
    Settings, then Integrations, then On this computer. While it is on,
    lastGLANCE listens only on your own computer (127.0.0.1) and gives the
    plugin what its keys show: your chores' names and icons and how long since
    each was done, the person at the top of Reach out, and your next birthday or
    date. From a key, the plugin can mark a chore done and open lastGLANCE at a
    chore, a person, or the new-chore form. The plugin runs inside Elgato's
    Stream Deck software on your computer, and neither lastGLANCE nor the plugin
    sends this data over the internet. As with any local connection, other
    software running on your computer could also connect while it is on.
    Elgato's privacy policy governs the Stream Deck software.
  </p>

  <h2>Third-party services</h2>
  <p>
    lastGLANCE does not integrate with any third-party analytics, advertising,
    or data brokers. The only third-party services involved are those you choose
    to use:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Apple iCloud (iPhone, iPad and Mac), your own account</li>
    <li>Your WebDAV / Nextcloud sync server</li>
    <li>A GLANCEvault instance you run</li>
    <li>The tool that keeps your sync folder in step, if you use folder sync</li>
    <li>Your phone's dialer, messaging and mail apps, when you tap Call, Text or Email</li>
    <li>
      Any MCP client application you connect, only if you enable the optional
      MCP server in the desktop app: the client reads lastGLANCE data locally and
      commonly transmits what it reads to its own AI provider
    </li>
    <li>
      Elgato Stream Deck software, only if you use the lastGLANCE Stream Deck
      plugin: the data it shows stays on your computer
    </li>
    <li>Apple App Store or Google Play (purchases)</li>
    <li>RevenueCat (purchase status, on Apple platforms only)</li>
    <li>
      GitHub (api.github.com): the desktop app downloaded from GitHub checks for
      new releases; no personal data is sent
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Purchases</h2>
  <p>
    lastGLANCE offers an optional unlock. On Android it is processed entirely by
    Google Play; on iPhone, iPad and the Mac App Store version it is processed
    entirely by the Apple App Store. We do not see or store your payment
    information. On Android, purchase status is checked on your device through
    Google Play and is not sent to us. On Apple platforms, lastGLANCE uses
    RevenueCat to manage the unlock: RevenueCat receives an anonymized app-user
    identifier and your Apple purchase receipt, from which it determines whether
    you have unlocked the app. It does not receive your name, your app content,
    or other personally identifying information. The versions of lastGLANCE
    downloaded from GitHub (Android and desktop) and the web app have no
    paywall. The applicable store's privacy policy governs payment processing,
    and RevenueCat's privacy policy governs its handling of this data.
  </p>

  <ChildrensPrivacy />

  <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes materially, we will update the "Last updated" date
    above and note the change in the app's release notes.
  </p>

  <PrivacyContact />
</ProseLayout>
